{"cut_proposal":{"candidate_errors":[],"prefix":"To solve the given equation \\(xy(x^2 - y^2) = x^2 + y^2\\), we start by rewriting it in a more convenient form. First, we can expand and rearrange the terms:\n\n\\[xy(x^2 - y^2) = x^2 + y^2 \\implies x^3y - xy^3 = x^2 + y^2.\\]\n\nNext, we will consider the possibility of setting \\(x = y\\) or \\(x = -y\\) to simplify the equation. However, since \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) are nonzero and the equation is symmetric, we will try to find a general solution. Let's divide both sides of the equation by \\(y^2\\) (assuming \\(y \\neq 0\\)):\n\n\\[x \\left( \\frac{x}{y} \\right) \\left( x^2 - y^2 \\right) = x^2 + y^2 \\implies x \\left( \\frac{x}{y} \\right) \\left( \\left( \\frac{x}{y} \\right)^2 - 1 \\right) = \\left( \\frac{x}{y} \\right)^2 + 1.\\]\n\nLet \\(t = \\frac{x}{y}\\). Then the equation becomes:\n\n\\[t (t^2 - 1) = t^2 + 1 \\implies t^3 - t = t^2 + 1 \\implies t^3 - t^2 - t - 1 = 0.\\]\n\nWe can test possible rational roots of the polynomial \\(t^3 - t^2 - t - 1 = 0\\). Testing \\(t = -1\\):\n\n\\[\n(-1)^3 - (-1)^2 - (-1) - 1 = -1 - 1 + 1 - 1 = -2 \\neq 0.\n\\]\n\nTesting \\(t = 1\\):\n\n\\[\n1^3 - 1^2 - 1 - 1 = 1 - 1 - 1 - 1 = -2 \\neq 0.\n\\]\n\nTesting \\(t = -\\frac{1}{2}\\):\n\n","prefix_end":1117,"status":"review_required"},"eligible":true,"generation":{"generation":{"max_new_tokens":2048,"temperature":0.7,"top_k":0,"top_p":1.0},"ids":[1249,11625,279,2661,23606,17767,4130,2075,61,17,481,379,61,17,8,284,856,61,17,488,379,61,17,59,701,582,1191,553,94445,432,304,264,803,16710,1352,13,5512,11,582,646,9225,323,55327,844,279,3793,1447,78045,4130,2075,61,17,481,379,61,17,8,284,856,61,17,488,379,61,17,1124,6383,550,856,61,18,88,481,30784,61,18,284,856,61,17,488,379,61,17,7110,2533,5847,11,582,686,2908,279,13038,315,6243,17767,87,284,379,57758,476,17767,87,284,481,88,57758,311,39721,279,23606,13,4354,11,2474,17767,87,57758,323,17767,88,57758,525,72232,323,279,23606,374,54343,11,582,686,1430,311,1477,264,4586,6291,13,6771